    Call 5: ('Evaluate three alternate fixes for the repeated ActivateSession intrusive-list corruption in open62541/src/server/ua_services_session.c: (1) on every successful activation, detach whenever session->header.channel is non-NULL (including the same channel), then unconditionally attach; (2) skip attach for same-channel activation; (3) replace intrusive membership with a separate allocated channel-list record or add explicit membership state. Which is most compatible with this old open62541 code and likely root-cause fix? Analyze whether detach-then-reattach on the same channel is safe and preserves ordering/semantics.', "The supplied vulnerability explicitly identifies Service_ActivateSession's condition `session->header.channel && session->header.channel != channel` as the defect because attach is unconditional. I tried making attach idempotent, also skipping attach in the service, and defensive cycle repair in detach, but the test system continues reporting the original UAF. I now suspect the intended and most direct correction is to change Service_ActivateSession so any existing attachment is detached before the existing unconditional attach. This guarantees the intrusive entry is removed and reinserted exactly once for both same-channel reactivation and migration, without changing successful activation, access-control callback, lifetime refresh, or logging.")...
    Call 6: ('Consider three alternate architectural fixes for the UAF in open62541/src/ua_securechannel.c cleanup: (1) make UA_SecureChannel_deleteMembersCleanup simply LIST_INIT channel->sessions without dereferencing entries; (2) in UA_SecureChannelManager_deleteMembers, scan the authoritative UA_SessionManager list and clear/detach sessions bound to each channel before freeing channels, then clear channel list without traversal; (3) redesign channel->sessions as separately allocated non-intrusive membership records. Which is safest and what exact structs/function signatures permit implementation? Account for normal runtime channel closure and later session reactivation, not just server shutdown.', "Multiple direct activation and detach fixes continue to trigger the same test dedup, so I need an alternate ownership solution that prevents channel cleanup from ever dereferencing potentially freed intrusive entries. A bare LIST_INIT avoids the ASan read but can leave live sessions' header.channel dangling after normal channel closure. The session manager is authoritative for live sessions, so scanning it by channel identity before channel destruction could clear backpointers without following the corrupted channel list. Need determine whether UA_SecureChannelManager has access to UA_Server/sessionManager and whether removeSecureChannelCallback can perform the same authoritative scan.")...
    Call 7: ('Could this open62541 repository build fuzz_binary_message from an amalgamated source copy rather than open62541/src/*.c? Locate all amalgamation/generated copies of Service_ActivateSession and UA_SecureChannel_deleteMembersCleanup and determine CMake source selection for tests/fuzz/fuzz_binary_message.cc. The repeated validator dedup appears unaffected even after removing the dangerous traversal from src/ua_securechannel.c.', "I replaced UA_SecureChannel_deleteMembersCleanup's traversal of channel->sessions with LIST_INIT, and added session-manager authoritative detachment before cleanup in ua_securechannel_manager.c. Yet test_patch reports exactly the old dedup. Earlier activation changes likewise seemed ineffective. This strongly suggests the fuzz target may compile an amalgamated open62541.c or another source copy. Need exact paths, build options, and which editable source should receive the patch.")...
